                           Emacs Web Server

    Copyright (C) 2026 OC (cosmicz@github)
    Modified by OC (cosmicz@github), 2026: Document complete-body framing, finite admission limits, lazy expiry, and bind exposure. Also document DISPATCHED REQUEST LIFETIME. Document private framing ownership and removal of unsupported accessors.

DESCRIPTION
    A web server in Emacs running handlers written in Emacs Lisp.

REQUIREMENTS
    Emacs 24.3 or later.

STATUS
    Supports HTTP GET and POST requests including URL-encoded
    parameters, multipart/form data and file uploads.  Supports web
    sockets.  Reasonably performant, faster than Elnode [1].  This is
    a new project without much extended use so there are likely bugs
    and potentially security issues.  That said it consists of little
    more than HTTP header parsing logic perched atop Emacs' existing
    network process primitives, so it should be fairly robust.

    [1]  http://eschulte.github.io/emacs-web-server/benchmark/


PRIVATE FRAMING STATE
    The unsupported public-looking ws-frame-* slot accessor functions have
    been removed, with no compatibility aliases. Code that used those
    internals must migrate; live migration of already loaded old objects
    is not provided. The supported ws-request-target, ws-request-raw-query
    and ws-request-form-params getters and framing configuration remain.
    Internal ws--frame-* names are private by convention, not Lisp-enforced
    secrecy. Server admission/sentinel entries are separate from retained
    request framing and accepted-message records. Limits are validated once
    and shared without mutation. Zero-input expiry is admission + timeout;
    after first input the request owns one fixed first-input + timeout
    deadline, read by both input refusal and lazy admission reclaim.


REQUEST FRAMING
    Requests are dispatched only after the complete Content-Length body has
    arrived, for every content type.  Missing Content-Length means an empty
    body; coalesced undeclared bytes are refused.  Request transfer codings
    (including chunked) are unsupported (501).  Invalid or ambiguous framing
    is refused (400), headers above the ceiling give 431, bodies above the
    absolute ceiling give 413, and late input gives 408.  These transport
    refusals occur before application handlers or authentication.

    ws-start validates and captures ws-request-body-limit (8388608 octets),
    ws-request-header-limit (65536 octets, including request line and all of
    CRLFCRLF), ws-request-framing-timeout (30 positive finite seconds), and
    ws-request-incomplete-limit (16 positive integer sockets).  There is no
    unlimited sentinel.  Changing globals does not change an existing server.
    The cap includes accepted zero-byte sockets and partial headers/bodies.
    The default retained pending-string ceiling is 16*(8388608+65536), or
    135266304 octets (129 MiB); metadata, socket/OS storage and completed body
    copies are additional.  Deferred, streaming, WebSocket lifetimes and the
    kernel backlog are outside this framing cap.

    Expiry is LAZY: there is no timer promising to close every quiet peer at
    30 seconds.  First input fixes a completion deadline that later bytes
    never renew; expiry is checked on later input before dispatch.  On a new
    accept, expired incomplete peers are silently reclaimed before the cap
    is checked.  Before first input their clock is admission time; afterward
    it is first-input time.  At a full cap new peers are silently refused
    until a slot is released or an entry expires against its applicable
    timestamp (at most one interval after admission or first input).  Without
    further input/admission, quiet entries can remain until EOF or stop.
    Unexpired, dispatched and deferred responses are never pressure-evicted.

    Clime defaults to 127.0.0.1:8080 and explicitly passes :host.  A wider
    :host exposes this pre-authentication framing/refusal path more widely;
    authentication does not reserve framing capacity.  Choose binds and
    limits for the intended exposure.  EOF, refusal, completion and stop
    release framing ownership; optional logging failure does not revoke a
    successful admission.  Caller sentinels keep their native accept/close
    events, including deleted then open-on-dead for a cap-refused accept.

    Complete-body framing reimplements the idea discussed in upstream PR #31
    (dickmao) for all content types and octets; that patch is not imported.

DISPATCHED REQUEST LIFETIME
    A complete non-deferred handler returning :keep-alive (or throwing it
    to close-connection) retains its request while the connection is live.
    After the handler returns, a sentinel prunes that exact request on TCP
    close, including WebSocket upgrades that replace the process plist.
    Deferred responders and incomplete framing retain their own sentinels.
    WebSocket CLOSE-frame protocol behavior is unchanged.

    A sentinel installed by the handler receives its actual events; terminal
    detachment happens before that callback. A caller replacing the sentinel
    afterward must chain the captured prior sentinel to retain this cleanup.
    ws-stop detaches all requests, including already-dead entries, before
    closing callbacks and completes pending responders as stopped once.
    Caller-held request objects remain usable. This is collection pruning,
    not a garbage-collection guarantee or a general memory bound.

USAGE
    See the examples/ directory in this repository for examples
    demonstrating usage.  The Emacs web-server is also used to run a
    paste server [2], serve editable Org-mode pages [3] and serve
    files for Cask [4].

    [2]  https://github.com/eschulte/el-sprunge
    [3]  https://github.com/eschulte/org-ehtml
    [4]  https://github.com/cask/cask

    Available from the GNU ELPA [5].  The tutorials page [6] walks
    through usage scenarios including installing the Emacs web-server
    and running it behind a proxy.

    [5]  http://elpa.gnu.org/
    [6]  http://eschulte.github.io/emacs-web-server/tutorials/

    Run `make check' to run the included test suite.

DOCUMENTATION
    Run `make doc' to build the texinfo documentation, also available
    online [6].

    [6]  http://eschulte.github.io/emacs-web-server
